      "notes": [
        "(For students admitted in 2026-27 under the 4-year degree) Extended Major Program in Creative Arts and Digital Humanities Extended Major is an add-on element to enrich the existing majors. Students should declare their Extended Major via the Opt-in Arrangement after they have been admitted to HKUST. Students who wish to withdraw from the Extended Major should apply before the last day of the add/drop period in the first regular term of their final year of study. The Extended Major in Creative Arts and Digital Humanities is available for combination with Majors offered by School of Science (BSc programs in Mathematics, and Ocean Science and Technology), Majors offered by School of Business and Management (BBA/BSc programs in Economics, Economics and Finance, Finance, Global Business, Information Systems, Management, Marketing, Operations Management, Professional Accounting, Quantitative Finance, Risk Management and Business Intelligence, and Sustainable and Green Finance), Majors offered by School of Humanities and Social Science (BSc programs in Global China Studies, and Quantitative Social Analysis), and BSc program in Innovation, Design and Technology offered by the Academy of Interdisciplinary Studies. It is designed for students with fundamental knowledge in calculus and programming, but also open to other students, given that they may be required to take one or two additional courses to acquire relevant foundation. To graduate with an Extended Major, students must have enrolled in the Extended Major, complete a minimum of 21 credits and all of its requirements, as well as the requirements of the major program of study; and have attained an average grade point of at least 2.15 in courses taken within the Extended Major. All courses counted towards the Extended Major Requirements, as well as those used to fulfill the Major Requirements, will be included in the calculation of the Major Cumulative Grade Average (MCGA). Students must take all the Extended Major requirement, within which they must complete at least 12 single- counted credits. These 12 credits cannot be used to fulfill any other requirements for graduation except for the 120-credit degree requirement. For credit transfer, students can transfer a maximum total of 6 credits to the Extended Major program. Students may use no more than 9 credits earned from courses delivered in online mode, including HKUST self-paced online [SPO] courses and other online credits approved through the University’s credit transfer procedures, to satisfy the graduation requirements of a degree program. Under the new 30-credit Common Core Program which is applicable to students admitted to the University in 2022-23 and thereafter, courses that have been counted towards School and/or Major Requirements are not allowed to be reused for fulfilment of the University Common Core Requirements. Students should look up the details of the Common Core Program including the general and School-/program-specific distributional requirements posted on the Common Core website where the link to it is available on this website. Extended Major Requirements",
